  "style": "",
  "text": "Non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) accounts for roughly 85% of all lung cancer cases and substantially contributes to cancer deaths worldwide . Both immune checkpoint blockade (ICB) immunotherapy and radiotherapy are first-line treatments for NSCLC. For locally advanced NSCLC, chemoradiation combined with ICB has become the standard treatment. [1–4] However, the curative efficacy of radio-immunotherapy is still suboptimal, and radioresistance remains a significant barrier to improving the antitumor-immunity of IR, limiting the survival benefit of NSCLC from radiotherapy–immunotherapy combinations [5]. Therefore, there is an urgent need to investigate prospective combination modalities to enhance the antitumor immunity of IR."
}

